'' 'a as integer = 123' inside a TYPE. The parser recorded the value on the field's
'' node and NOTHING downstream read it back: the field was laid out at the right offset
'' and then initialised by TYPE alone, so 'dim as UDT v' printed whatever the stack held
'' where fbc prints 123, and 'dim shared as UDT g' printed 0. No diagnostic either way.
''
'' Three separate gaps, and closing any two of them still printed garbage:
''
''   the NAME-FIRST parser branch dropped the '= value' entirely -- only 'as integer i =
''   1' kept it, and 'i as integer = 1' is the spelling everyone writes;
''
''   Resolve never walked the expression, so every NAME inside one was unbound -- and
''   lowering did not decline on that, it lowered the unbound thing: '= f( )' and '= g'
''   each stored a garbage word;
''
''   TypeNeedsInit_ never counted an initialiser as work, so an all-scalar UDT got no
''   synthesised constructor at all and there was no prologue for the stores to go in.
''
'' Measured against fbc, which prints '123 1.5 18 hi 42 5 / abc / 123 7' for the source
'' below -- the same line tiko-lang now prints.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/field-init.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: func void _ZN3UDTC1Ev(ptr $0) {
'' A CONSTANT, A DOUBLE, A CONST EXPRESSION, A CALL AND A GLOBAL -- the three that need
'' Resolve are the three that were silently wrong.
'' EXPECT-OUT: const f64 1.5
'' EXPECT-OUT: call i64 F()
'' EXPECT-OUT: load @@g
'' A VAR-LENGTH STRING IS ASSIGNED, and a 'zstring * 8' is assigned with its OWN length
'' rather than -1: it is an ARRAY of TY_ZSTRING, which IsStringType says no to, and that
'' is what sent it to the aggregate decline.
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_StrAssign(%9, -1, @@$str2, 3, 0)
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_StrAssign(%18, 8, @@$str3, 4, 0)
'' THE TYPE-FIRST SPELLING gets its own constructor for the same reason.
'' EXPECT-OUT: func void _ZN5OTHERC1Ev(ptr $0) {
'' AND A BRACE LIST OVER AN ARRAY FIELD -- the same element walk a local array
'' declaration does, which took a frame SLOT and now takes an address as well.
'' EXPECT-OUT: func void _ZN4ARRSC1Ev(ptr $0) {
'' EXPECT-OUT: const i64 17
'' GOLDEN: expected/field-init.out
